# Line endings (ruling R149).
#
# core.autocrlf=true normalises to LF in the index on commit and converts to
# CRLF on checkout. The index is already LF, so commits were never the problem:
# the exposure was the other direction. A fresh clone or `git checkout --` would
# have written CRLF into the WORKING TREE, which is where every hygiene check on
# this project reads, so the "no CRLF" rule was unenforced against checkout.
#
# eol=lf pins LF on both sides and makes that rule actually enforceable. It was
# verified to be a no-op for existing content before being added: all 62 tracked
# files already store LF in HEAD, so this creates no phantom diff.
#
# text=auto still detects binary by content, so a binary file added later is
# left alone without needing a rule of its own.
* text=auto eol=lf
